A||||13||BroCar 87||More Quad 4 questions...||||5||
Z||000000||BroCar 87||10-29-2000||12:16 AM||||I can still get a Quad 4 engine cheap! It is in the car still and runs. If I were to swap this engine into my fiero, could I replace the regular Quad 4 with a H.O. Quad 4?<BR>The engine I can get is out of a Grand Am, and I don't know if it is a H.O. or not. I am asking because my lil "Iron Beast" is slowly dying  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Anyone know about this swap? Regular Quad 4 to H.O. Quad 4!!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature2.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>   Custom dual side exit exhaust!||||reg||5
Z||000001||BroCar 87||10-29-2000||03:07 AM||||To me, it looks as if the H.O. is the same motor with better "guts". If I were to rebuild this regular Quad 4, Could I rebuild it with the H.O. components?? <BR>Are they the same block, heads, and exterior engine components????<P>ANY help?<BR>Thanks<BR>Bro out!!!||||reg||4
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-29-2000||12:25 PM||||Joe Wynman did a lot with Quad 4 swaps into Fieros. fierojoe@bellatlantic.net<P>Joe is one of the techincal writers for "High Performance Pontiac" magazine and owns Associated Auto Inc.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fieroboy1||10-29-2000||05:27 PM||||A couple of years ago i researched this swap quite a bit and from what i could gather the non h.o. has lower compression and less aggressive camming.  I wouldn't bother with a non h.o. motor to be homest with you.  Non H.o's make like 150 hp or 140 hp not quite sure of the numbers.  Swapping a quad 4 is a rather intense swap needing adapters and a lot of wiring splicing. If you are going to the trouble you might as well put in an H.O. To tell if it's h.o. or not look on the cover on the top of the engine it will say h.o.  Beretta gtz's had them, and a couple of others...||||reg||1
Z||000004||BroCar 87||10-29-2000||05:58 PM||||Thanks for the insight guys, There is a local shop that deals alot with the Quad 4's, so I don't believe I will be doing it myself. SPEED CRAFT / CHASSIS CRAFT is the names of the shops down here!<BR>Fieroboy: 150 HP... isn't that better than the iron dukes 90 HP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Also, if it isn't a H.O., If/when I rebuild the Quad 4 can I rebuild it with the better "guts" of the H.O.??????<BR>Thanks again!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature2.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>   Custom dual side exit exhaust!||||reg||10
Z||000005||Ed||10-29-2000||06:37 PM||||I'm also following this as I'm still debating about making an offer on a rebodied car with an '88 quad four in it.  I've heard this is supposed to make about 160 hp (this is not the HO later model).  <P>I also understand that the engine is lighter than the 2.8L V-6 so both the specific output and the gross power to weight ratio of the complete car ought to be better.   <P>This has to be compared to the 3.1 and 3.4 swaps.  <P>So the trades are for a higher revving, lighter four, versus a more more modern V-6 with more low end torque, more absolute hp (at least for the 3.4), but more weight than the quad four, but similar to what we already have.<P>Any other thoughts on the pros and cons?  I've driven the car and it seems to handle better and go faster.  I don't have any hard test track data, though.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fieroboy1||10-29-2000||09:17 PM||||As far as rebuilding non h.o. quads to h.o. spec i'm not sure how involved it is, I'm not sure how they attain the higher compression for the h.o. motor, it may be domed pistons, or slightly different head configuration, and i know that the h.o is all forged construction, crank, pistons. You are right in saying the 150 hp is more than the dukes but my point is if you are going to pull your hair out to do a fairly difficult swap, why not hold out for the H.O.  Good luck, i always wanted a quad 4 fiero.  I'll have to settle for my 95 camaro 3.4 motor.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ed||10-29-2000||10:33 PM||||Fieroboy1:<P>Why would you want a quad 4 and have to "settle" for a 3.4L?<P>That is part of my question of quad 4 v. 3.4L. You must see something about the quad 4 which is appealing.  What do you like about the quad 4?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||BroCar 87||10-30-2000||02:33 AM||||Ed... actually the Quad 4 is lighter than the Iron duke as well. It has a greater 50/50 weight distribution on the car. <BR>Fieroboy... the reason i want to do this swap so quickly is because my engine is slowly dying. It just broke down again this past saturday on the way to the first annual fiero meet in Jax.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I haven't found out whats wrong yet! So I am doing as much research as possible. <BR>Thanks for the help so far... its a great thing to know that more people than just me are into the Quad 4 swap.<p>[This message has been edited by BroCar 87 (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000009||Eric_PA||10-30-2000||05:36 AM||||I have to jump in here and support the Quad 4 swap. I have a HO Quad setting in the garage now for over a year. I got it from Kick Hill Farms and still haven't found the time to get it in. I think that this engine has a lot of potential. I recently have been confused as to what to do. I have an Automatic GT Fastback and was considering a 3.4 swap. What do you think would perform better. A 3.4 with the three speed automatic or the HO Quad with a 5 speed. Another problem I am having is deciding to pay the $1800 from ACE for the kit. The kit doesn't include the brackets needed to convert the trans. You have to locate a Fiero Getrag bracket for them to modify for you. I hope this winter to get the engine in for the Spring. That is if I decide to still put it in since everyday I change my mind on what to do to this car. It is hard to rip out a perfectly good running engine, especially a v6 and swap it for a four bamger.<P>Eric<BR>Black 86.5 GT||||reg||10
Z||000010||Ed||10-30-2000||02:18 PM||||As stated above, I'm also debating getting an upgraded V-6 (I already have a fine running '87 GT) versus getting an already built-up kit car with a Quad 4.<P>Well, now I'm convinced that the Quad 4 is a good swap.  I read last night (before your post, brother) about the engine weight being even less than the Duke.  The car I'm looking at only has the 160 hp engine (only!), but the gross power to weight ratio would be better than even some of the V-6 upgrades.  <P>The car is complete, I've driven it, its been serviced by a dealer(!), and the engine was professionally installed.  I've had both the Duke and the V-6, and I feel the lighter car is better handling (the usual situation).<P>I guess its time to get serious about negotiating a price.||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||10-30-2000||02:27 PM||||Ed,<P>I just wanted to clear some things up about your quote...<P>"So the trades are for a higher revving, lighter four, versus a more more modern V-6 "<P>... although chronologically the 3.4L V-6 is newer, technologically speaking this is not the case.  The Quad 4 was the most technological engine in the GM parts barn from the 80's.  This engine was the starting point for the later 3.4L TDC and also the modern day Northstar.  Therefore, the Quad 4 is much more technolically advanced than the older pushrod design of the 3.4L.  <P>The engine IS not as bad as many claim and most of the original design and geometry from the original Quad's is still used in the Northstar.  The problem with the Quads was in owner maintenance.  A US DOHC is a bit of a finicky beast.  Americans are more used to the bullet-proof pushrod design which resist much more abuse (maintenance-wise).  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000012||Fieroboy1||10-30-2000||08:29 PM||||Ed, when i said i was settling for the 3.4 it was mostly in jest.  My 86 GT is an auto so i would prefer the higher torque ratings of the 3.4.  The pro's of a quad 4 are what bro car mentioned about weight distribution.  You are dealing with an engine that has 60-80 more hp than the duke and weighs less due to the aluminum construction it employs.  A 5 spd quad 4 is the most true to what i feel a mid engine car should be, high revving and nimble with good weight distribution. A big v-6 makes for a good cruising GT (grand touring) vehicle that doesn't require revving and gear selection to haul ass. Just my 2 cents.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ed||10-30-2000||08:49 PM||||Thanks for the positive inputs from all.  <P>I'll give the owner a call tonight and resume negotiations.  <P>Anyone want to buy a really well kept up '87 GT?  <P>Wait!  After I get the Ferrino and check it out!  ||||reg||1
